\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Away restricted products and finite-prime splitting}

\p{Use the Hausdorff locally compact groups #{G_p} and compact open
references #{U_p} of \ref{fcap-004F}. For any set #{R} of rational
primes, the \newvocab{away restricted product} is

##{G_f^R(U)=\prod_{p\notin R}'(G_p,U_p).}

Its tuples have only finitely many exceptions to #{g_p\in U_p}
among the primes outside #{R}. Its topology is defined by the same
principal-stage construction, now indexed by finite sets
#{T\subseteq\{p:p\notin R\}}:

##{G_T^R(U)=\prod_{p\in T}G_p\times\prod_{p\notin R\cup T}U_p.}

The proof in \ref{fcap-004F} applies to this index set too. Thus
#{G_f^R(U)} is a Hausdorff locally compact topological group, and
each displayed stage is open with its product topology.}

\p{Deleting the coordinates in #{R} defines a continuous group
homomorphism

##{\begin{aligned}
r_R:G_f(U)&\longrightarrow G_f^R(U),\\
(g_p)_p&\longmapsto(g_p)_{p\notin R}.
\end{aligned}}

Indeed, a finite exceptional set #{S} restricts to #{S\setminus R},
so the map is well defined. On the open stage #{G_S(U)} it is
the product-coordinate restriction
#{G_S(U)\to G_{S\setminus R}^R(U)}, followed by the continuous
stage inclusion. These restrictions are continuous, and the stages
cover the source, proving continuity of #{r_R}. Coordinatewise
operations prove the homomorphism assertion.}

\p{For #{R=\varnothing}, the away group and restriction are
#{G_f(U)} and its identity map. If #{R} is the set of all primes,
the complement is empty, so the away group is the one-point group
and restriction is its unique incoming homomorphism. No finiteness
of #{R} is needed for the definition or for continuity.}

\p{If #{R} is finite, separating its coordinates gives the
\newvocab{finite-prime splitting}, a topological group isomorphism

##{\begin{aligned}
d_R:G_f(U)&\longrightarrow
\Bigl(\prod_{p\in R}G_p\Bigr)\times G_f^R(U),\\
(g_p)_p&\longmapsto\bigl((g_p)_{p\in R},r_R(g)\bigr).
\end{aligned}}

The target carries the product topology. In particular the removed
prime factors are unrestricted. For the hyperbolic-plane tuple in
\ref{fcap-004F}, choosing #{R=\{2\}} gives
#{d_R(g)=(\operatorname{diag}(2,1/2),1)}. Reinserting that component
recovers the tuple, even though its #{2}-component is outside #{U_2}.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{The inverse of #{d_R} reinserts the coordinates indexed by #{R}.
If the away tuple has finite exceptional set #{T}, the reconstructed
tuple has exceptional set contained in #{R\cup T}. This is finite,
so the inverse is well defined. Coordinate restriction and reinsertion
are mutually inverse homomorphisms. Continuity of #{d_R} follows
from the continuous coordinate evaluations of \ref{fcap-004F} and
the continuous map #{r_R}.}

\p{For the inverse, the open subsets

##{\Bigl(\prod_{p\in R}G_p\Bigr)\times G_T^R(U)}

cover the target as #{T} ranges over the finite subsets of the
complement. On each such subset reinsertion is the product
rearrangement onto #{G_{R\cup T}(U)}, followed by its continuous
inclusion into #{G_f(U)}. Hence the inverse is continuous on an
open cover, and therefore continuous everywhere. This argument uses
the established open stages and the ordinary product topology.}
}

\p{If #{R} is infinite, the removed coordinates must retain their
own restricted-product condition. An unrestricted family on #{R}
can have infinitely many components outside #{U_p}; reinserting
it would not define a point of #{G_f(U)}. Thus the finite-prime
formula does not extend by simply allowing infinite #{R}.
The appropriate removed-coordinate factor is
#{\prod_{p\in R}'(G_p,U_p)}. At the level of tuples this and the
away factor split the finite exceptional set into two finite sets;
conversely their union is finite. The resulting group isomorphism
is also a homeomorphism: on each open product of two principal
stages, reinsertion is a product rearrangement into the stage
indexed by their union; restriction is continuous on every source
stage. This also covers the empty and all-prime boundaries.}

\p{For a \vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuple}{fcap-004C},
the same construction defines #{O_f^R(U)}, #{SO_f^R(U)} and
#{\Spin_f^R(U)} by taking the local families #{O_p}, #{SO_p} and
#{\Spin_p} and their respective reference subgroups.
The finite-prime splitting is a direct consequence of the stage
topology; the classical affine adelic finite-stage decomposition is
\citet{Theorem 3.6, equation (3.6.1), pp. 6-8}{conrad2011adelic}.}

\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.RestrictedProductGroupAway}
defines the arbitrary-set carrier, and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.restrictAway} with
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.continuous_restrictAway} supplies restriction.
The finite-set splitting and its two continuity directions are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.awayDecomposition},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.continuous_awayDecomposition} and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.continuous_awayDecomposition_symm}.
Its forward continuity does not need open references; inverse
continuity assumes openness on the away coordinates, which our
compact-open tuple supplies. The three specialized carriers are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.awayAdelicOrthogonal},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.awayAdelicSpecialOrthogonal}
and \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.awayAdelicSpin}.}
